  "step_3": "The decision reason requires preferring the lottery with the higher probability of a favorable outcome, which would be Lottery B (25% vs. 20%). The individual, however, chose Lottery A and justified this by minimizing the importance of the 5% difference in probabilities. While the individual does reference the 5% difference in probabilities—indicating awareness of the relevant information—they do not apply the rule of the decision reason (i.e., choosing the lottery with the higher chance of winning). Instead, they appear to treat the probabilities as nearly equivalent and base the decision on something else (possibly outcome magnitude, though not stated). Therefore, although the individual considers probability information, they do not follow the decision rule prescribed by the reason. The justification runs contrary to the reason's preference for higher probability. Thus, it is unlikely the individual used this decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
